WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.788
ROLLUP creates hierarchical subtotals In this
example category is the higher-level

00:00:05.812 --> 00:00:10.188
dimension and product is nested underneath it The
query sums sales and

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:15.712
groups by ROLLUP category product PostgreSQL first
returns the most detailed

00:00:15.738 --> 00:00:21.338
groups one for each category-and-product combination
It then produces a subtotal for

00:00:21.362 --> 00:00:25.550
each category and finally a grand total across all
categories and products

00:00:26.425 --> 00:00:31.338
Subtotal rows need special labels because PostgreSQL
uses NULL-like placeholders in rolled-up

00:00:31.362 --> 00:00:35.675
columns The GROUPING function tells us whether a
column is missing because

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:38.800
it was rolled up rather than because the source data
actually contained

00:00:38.825 --> 00:00:43.050
NULL The CASE expressions use GROUPING to display
labels such as All

00:00:43.075 --> 00:00:48.200
categories or All products The grouping_code is also
included so you can

00:00:48.225 --> 00:00:52.325
see which level each row represents Run the query
and find three

00:00:52.350 --> 00:00:56.850
kinds of rows detailed product rows category
subtotal rows and the grand

00:00:56.862 --> 00:01:01.062
total ROLLUP is a good fit for reports with a
hierarchy such

00:01:01.075 --> 00:01:05.400
as region to store category to product or year to
month The

00:01:05.425 --> 00:01:08.550
order of columns matters because it defines the
rollup path
